    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4230A\n\nSPONSOR: KAMINSKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the real property tax law, in relation\nto excluding water system property owned by water-works corporations\nfrom special franchise tax\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nAmends the Real Property Tax Law to exclude water system property owned\nby the water-works corporations from the special franchise tax.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 excludes water and water system property owned by a waterworks\ncorporation from the special franchise tax.\n\nSection 2 provides a 5 year phase out for the water tax.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPrivate water utilities serve approximately 4% of New Yorkers; the\nremaining 96% of New Yorkers are served by public water systems.\nPrivate water utilities pay income and property taxes, which are costs\nthat are directly and legally passed through to customers in rates. This\nhas contributed to significantly higher water utility rates for private\nwater utility customers, compared to those on public systems. For exam-\nple, 33% to 59% of New York American Water utility bill pays for proper-\nty taxes, and 17% to 23% of the Suez utility bill pays for property\ntaxes.\n\nSpecial Franchise taxes are taxes paid for utility assets located in\nmunicipal rights of way and represent a significant component of the\ntotal property taxes paid by water utilities. For example, New York\nAmerican Water pays approximately $25 million a year in Special Fran-\nchise taxes. This legislation will phase out the Special Franchise tax\nfor water utilities over five years. New York American Water will\ncontinue to pay over $12 million a year in property taxes on traditional\nreal estate and plant on private property. All savings that result from\nthe legislation must be used to reduce current water rates and offset\nfuture water rate increases for customers.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nSavings of $25 million a year to be passed onto cus